Modern approach — `Object.groupBy` (Node 21+, all current browsers):

```js
const people = [
  { name: 'Ada', dept: 'eng' },
  { name: 'Lin', dept: 'sales' },
  { name: 'Rai', dept: 'eng' },
];

const byDept = Object.groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
// { eng: [{name:'Ada',…}, {name:'Rai',…}], sales: [{name:'Lin',…}] }
```

The result is a `null`-prototype object, so `byDept.toString` won't collide with anything inherited.

If you need keys that aren't strings (dates, objects, numbers kept as numbers), use `Map.groupBy`:

```js
const byDept = Map.groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
byDept.get('eng'); // [{name:'Ada',…}, {name:'Rai',…}]
```

### Portable version

For older runtimes, a `reduce` does the same thing:

```js
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  return arr.reduce((acc, item) => {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    (acc[key] ??= []).push(item);
    return acc;
  }, Object.create(null));
}

const byDept = groupBy(people, p => p.dept);
```

Grouping by a property name rather than a function is just `groupBy(people, p => p.dept)` vs. passing `'dept'` — the callback form is worth keeping since it handles derived keys (`p => p.date.getFullYear()`) for free.

One gotcha with the plain-object form: keys are stringified, so `1` and `'1'` collapse into the same bucket, and numeric-looking keys get iterated in ascending numeric order rather than insertion order. `Map.groupBy` avoids both.

